WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.400
Think of cell signaling as switch logic.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.780
When a ligand binds its receptor, the switch flips on, and second messengers like

3
00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.800
cyclic AMP, IP3, and calcium fan out through the cell.

4
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:24.520
But every switch that turns on must also turn off, through phosphatases that remove

5
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.620
phosphates and enzymes that destroy the messengers.

6
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.540
Hold on to that pairing.

7
00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.180
Almost every pathway in this chapter is the same four-beat sentence.

8
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:40.820
A switch turns on, the signal spreads and amplifies, the switch turns off, and when

9
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.260
the off step fails, the switch sticks.

10
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.100
And that is disease.

11
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:51.993
Every signal transduction pathway that starts at a membrane receptor shares the same

12
00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:58.693
molecular logic. A primary messenger, a hormone or neurotransmitter, is detected by

13
00:00:58.693 --> 00:01:00.273
a membrane-spanning receptor.

14
00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:06.753
The receptor generates or activates a second messenger inside the cell, which drives

15
00:01:06.753 --> 00:01:10.313
effector molecules, which produce the physiological response.

16
00:01:11.153 --> 00:01:14.653
Five parts in order, plus one that is easy to forget.

17
00:01:15.753 --> 00:01:16.753
Active termination.

18
00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:21.813
The pathway has to be switched off on purpose, or the signal never ends.

19
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:27.887
Before the receptor mechanisms, ask what is doing the signaling in the first place.

20
00:01:28.847 --> 00:01:34.607
First messengers are the extracellular signals, hormones, neurotransmitters, growth

21
00:01:34.607 --> 00:01:36.107
factors, even light.

22
00:01:36.907 --> 00:01:38.627
The key split is physical.

23
00:01:39.707 --> 00:01:44.667
Small lipophilic messengers like steroids slip straight through the membrane to

24
00:01:44.667 --> 00:01:46.207
receptors inside the cell.

25
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:52.747
Large or polar messengers like peptide hormones cannot cross, so they must be read

26
00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:56.407
by a receptor that spans the membrane and relays the news inward.

27
00:01:57.587 --> 00:02:00.307
Size and polarity decide where the receptor lives.

28
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:06.757
Glucagon and epinephrine are the classic first messengers for fuel mobilization.

29
00:02:07.777 --> 00:02:12.857
When blood glucose drops or the fight-or-flight response fires, these hormones

30
00:02:12.857 --> 00:02:18.217
launch a signaling cascade that hits fuel metabolism on several fronts at once.

31
00:02:18.877 --> 00:02:24.297
It breaks down glycogen, shuts off glycogen synthesis, and frees stored fat.

32
00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:30.577
One hormone arriving at the surface reorganizes the cell's entire energy strategy

33
00:02:30.577 --> 00:02:34.977
because the signal is amplified into a large, coordinated response.

34
00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:42.747
When cyclic AMP rises inside a cell, it acts as a master switch for fuel metabolism

35
00:02:42.747 --> 00:02:49.667
through protein kinase A. PKA hits several targets at once, it shuts down glycogen

36
00:02:49.667 --> 00:02:55.687
synthesis by inhibiting glycogen synthase, and it turns on glycogen breakdown by

37
00:02:55.687 --> 00:03:00.107
activating phosphorylase kinase, which activates glycogen phosphorylase.

38
00:03:00.107 --> 00:03:06.147
One second messenger, working through one kinase, flips the cell from storing fuel

39
00:03:06.147 --> 00:03:10.227
to releasing it, all by adding phosphates to the right enzymes.
